a=3$ and $b=2$."] [17592186059524 :question/problem "Given that $2 - 3 \\textrm{i} = \\dfrac{p - 2q \\textrm{i}}{1 + 2 \\textrm{i}}$, calculate $p$ and $q$."] [17592186059524 :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $1+2\\textrm{i}$ to get rid of the fraction and isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 - 3\\textrm{i} &= \\frac{p - 2q\\textrm{i}}{1 + 2\\textrm{i}} \\\\\n(2 - 3\\textrm{i})(1 + 2\\textrm{i}) &= p - 2q\\textrm{i} \\\\\n2 + 4\\textrm{i} - 3\\textrm{i} - 6\\textrm{i}^{2} &= p - 2q\\textrm{i} \\\\\n2 + \\textrm{i} - 6(-1) &= p - 2q\\textrm{i} \\\\\n8 + \\textrm{i} &= p - 2q\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get\n$8 = p$.\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get\n$1|=-2q; q|=-\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, $p=8$ and\n\n$$\nq = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"] [17592186059525 :question/problem "Given that $3+5\\textrm{i} = (p+\\textrm{i}q)(1+\\textrm{i})$, calculate $p$ and $q$."] [17592186059525 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $1+\\textrm{i}$ to isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 + 5\\textrm{i} &= (p + \\textrm{i}q)(1 + \\textrm{i}) \\\\\n\\frac{3 + 5\\textrm{i}}{1 + \\textrm{i}} &= p + \\textrm{i}q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((3 + 5\\textrm{i})(1 - \\textrm{i}))/((1 + \\textrm{i})(1 - \\textrm{i})) &= p + \\textrm{i}q \\\\\n\\frac{3 - 3\\textrm{i} + 5\\textrm{i} - 5\\textrm{i}^{2}}{1^{2} - \\textrm{i}^{2}} &= p + \\textrm{i}q \\\\\n(3 + 2\\textrm{i} - 5(-1))/(1 - (-1)) &= p + \\textrm{i}q \\\\\n\\frac{8 + 2\\textrm{i}}{2} &= p + \\textrm{i}q \\\\\n4 + \\textrm{i} &= p + \\textrm{i}q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $4=p$.\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $1=q$.\n\nTherefore, $p=4$ and $q=1$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186058982 17592186058983 17592186058984 17592186058985 17592186058986 17592186059090 17592186059091 17592186059092 17592186059093 17592186059094] "immutable_fields" [[17592186058981 :answer-field/correct] [17592186058981 :answer-field/id] [17592186058981 :answer-field/key] [17592186058981 :answer-field/type] [17592186059089 :answer-field/correct] [17592186059089 :answer-field/id] [17592186059089 :answer-field/key] [17592186059089 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
